{"id":"mid-century-medieval","title":"Mid-Century Medieval","url":"https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Mid-Century_Medieval","rev":239432,"fetched":"2026-10-16","lead":[{"t":"p","c":[{"t":"b","c":["Mid-Century Medieval"]}," (also known as ",{"t":"b","c":["Medieval Revival"]},") is an aesthetic that reimagines the visual iconography and fashion associated with the Middle Ages through kitschy mid-20th century designs. Originally emerging in the 1950s, the style rose to prominence in the ",{"t":"a","c":["psychedelic era"],"href":"https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Psychedelia"}," of the 1960s and 1970s, particularly in the U.S. and UK's ",{"t":"a","c":["Hippie"],"href":"https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Hippie"}," scene and ",{"t":"a","c":["Peacock Revolution"],"href":"https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Peacock_Revolution"},". The style was associated with psychedelic and progressive pop, folk and rock."]}],"sections":[{"title":"History","id":"history","blocks":[{"t":"p","c":["During the 1950s, elements inspired by Renaissance and Middle Ages iconography were recontextualized with contemporary ",{"t":"a","c":["kitschy"],"href":"https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Kitsch"}," designs which were often seen in media such as film and television, most notably in 1955's ",{"t":"i","c":["The Court Jester"]},". By the mid-to late 1960s, the psychedelic era sparked a fashion movement known as the ",{"t":"a","c":["Peacock Revolution"],"href":"https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Peacock_Revolution"}," which led to widely liberalized styles of dress. Inspiring revivals of previous styles such as ",{"t":"a","c":["Art Nouveau"],"id":"art-nouveau"},", ",{"t":"a","c":["Victorian"],"href":"https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Victorian"}," and ",{"t":"a","c":["Baroque"],"id":"baroque"}," fashions, along with the medieval era. In 1963, Phyllis and Ron Patterson held the Renaissance Pleasure Faire, an event meant to mimic an Elizabethan country fair, as a fundraiser for the radio station KPFK. It ended up being successful with eight thousand people attending the event, causing the rise of renaissance fairs, where people could participate in medieval-themed activities as a form as escapism."]}]},{"title":"Visuals","id":"visuals","blocks":[{"t":"p","c":["The style is characterized by kitschy imagery inspired by the Middle Ages. The color palette consists of bright hues, mostly reds, yellows, greens and blues."]}]},{"title":"Fashion","id":"fashion","blocks":[{"t":"p","c":["Mid-Century Medieval fashion is based on silhouettes from the Middle Ages, often combined with contemporary 60s/70s fashion elements."]},{"t":"ul","items":[{"c":["Flowing dresses/gowns"]},{"c":["Tunics"]},{"c":["Embroidery"]},{"c":["Cloaks"]},{"c":["Chokers"]},{"c":["Hoods and capes"]},{"c":["Trumpet sleeves"]},{"c":["Bodices"]},{"c":["Bell sleeves"]},{"c":["Short hemlines"]}]}]},{"title":"Music","id":"music","blocks":[{"t":"p","c":["During the 60s/70s, folk music had a resurgence as many artists used medieval instruments and motifs into their songs."]},{"t":"h","l":3,"c":["Songs"]},{"t":"ul","items":[{"c":["As Tears Go By by Marianne Faithful (1964)"]},{"c":["Lady Jane by the Rolling Stones (1966)"]},{"c":["Guinevere by Donovan (1966)"]},{"c":["Scarborough Fair / Canticle by Simon & Garfunkel (1966)"]},{"c":["In Another Land by the Rolling Stones (1967)"]},{"c":["Silas Stingy by the Who (1967)"]},{"c":["Another Time by Pearls Before Swine (1967)"]},{"c":["Painting Box by the Incredible String Band (1967)"]},{"c":["Wind Quartets by Tyrannosaurus Rex (1968)"]},{"c":["The Half-Remarkable Question by the Incredible String Band (1968)"]},{"c":["Pewter Suitor by Tyrannosaurus Rex (1969)"]},{"c":["Murdoch by Trees (1971)"]},{"c":["Diana by Comus (1971)"]},{"c":["Dancing with the Moonlit Knight by Genesis (1973)"]}]},{"t":"h","l":3,"c":["Artists"]},{"t":"ul","items":[{"c":["Fairport Convention"]},{"c":["Pentangle"]},{"c":["Melicorne"]},{"c":["Tyrannosaurus Rex"]},{"c":["Steeleye Span"]},{"c":["